# WO-L6 Level 1 on a Mac and on GitHub Pages

## Goal

Bryan can present from a Mac: `Play LOB Over.command` does on macOS what `Play LOB Over.cmd` does on Windows, and the showcase site carries a no-headset Level 1 whose Plan page hosts the game in the browser with a phone or second laptop as the Site seat by room code. The headset still joins only a launcher-started host (it speaks WebSocket to a host on the same network; Pages serves files only).

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/LEVEL1.md` ("Starting a game"), `Play LOB Over.cmd`, `Monitor/src/host/launch.ps1`, `Monitor/src/host/server.js`, `Monitor/web/play.html`, `Monitor/web/host.html` (the browser host and room codes), `Monitor/web/lib/transport.js`, `Tools/publish_showcase.ps1`, `Showcase/`

## You may change

`Play LOB Over.command` (new, repo root), `Monitor/src/host/**`, `Monitor/web/**`, `Monitor/e2e/**`, `Monitor/test/**`, `Showcase/**`, `Tools/publish_showcase.ps1`, `README.md`. Not the simulation, the protocol, the scenarios or `Assets/`.

## Build

1. **`Play LOB Over.command`**, bash, committed with the executable bit (`git update-index --chmod=+x`): cd to its own folder; find node on PATH, `/opt/homebrew/bin` or `/usr/local/bin`, else print "Install Node.js 20 or newer from https://nodejs.org" and wait for a key; `npm ci` in `Monitor` when `node_modules` is missing; `adb reverse tcp:7331 tcp:7331` for each device `adb devices` lists (PATH or `~/Library/Android/sdk/platform-tools`), errors ignored; start `node Monitor/src/host/server.js Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json --discovery`; once port 7331 answers, open `http://localhost:7331/play.html` with `open` (macOS), `xdg-open` (Linux) or `start` (Git Bash on Windows, so the orchestrator can test it); print the machine's IPv4 addresses and "Press Ctrl-C to end the game".
2. **README.md** section "Presenting from a Mac": clone with git (a downloaded zip carries Gatekeeper's quarantine; if the file will not open, right-click > Open, or run `bash "Play LOB Over.command"` in Terminal); allow node through the macOS firewall prompt; use a phone hotspot or the USB cable when the venue's Wi-Fi blocks discovery; `brew install android-platform-tools` for the cable.
3. **Pages.** `play.html` gains a browser-host mode when opened with `?host=browser`, and the showcase's index links it as "Level 1, no headset": the page runs `createHost` with `level1_texas` itself, Start hosts the game, a room code with a QR lets a phone or second laptop open `site.html` as the Site seat (the slice's room mechanism), voices from the published `Voice/level1_texas/`, and a one-line banner "The headset joins only a game started with Play LOB Over on a PC or Mac". `Showcase/dist` includes `play.html`, `img/` and `Voice/level1_texas/`.
4. **Tests.** `bash -n` on the `.command`; an e2e that runs `play.html?host=browser` (Start, Hold, Go, result) with `site.html` joined by room code in a second browser context; the existing Level 1 e2e unchanged.

## Acceptance checks

- `npm test` and the e2e pass; `bash "Play LOB Over.command"` from Git Bash on the PC starts the host and opens the page; after `Tools/publish_showcase.ps1`, the published site answers 200 for `play.html` and `Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json`, and the live page in Chromium runs Level 1 to a result in browser-host mode.
- The Mac itself is checked by Bryan.

## Out of scope

The headset, the simulation, the stages' pages.
# WO-L2 Level 1 in the headset

## Goal

`Assets/LOBOver/Scenes/Play.unity`, made by Bryan from Building Blocks, becomes the headset's Level 1 seat. It starts in full passthrough with one readable panel, finds the PC's game by itself, warps to a chosen crew and back with a shader dissolve, and shows and plays the live game from the host. No menus, no modes, no embedded simulation, no truck. Accepted only after Bryan's headset check (K35).

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/LEVEL1.md` (all of it), WO-L1 (host, discovery packet, voice turns), `Docs/PROTOCOL.md`, `Docs/SITE_SEAT_V2.md` ("XR setup rules", the warp and comfort notes; its table model, standee, stake markers and wrist panel are not in Level 1)
- `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/{SiteSceneBuilder,BuildingBlocksSetup,QuestBuild}.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`
- The Interaction SDK's `RayInteractable`, `PokeInteractable` and `PointableCanvas`

## Precondition

`Play.unity` exists, made by Bryan from an empty scene with these blocks only: Camera Rig, Passthrough, Controller Tracking, Hand Tracking, Interactions Rig. If it is missing, stop and ask him. `Site.unity` stays as it is and leaves the build list.

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/**`, `Assets/Plugins/Android/AndroidManifest.xml`, `ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset` (Play.unity only), and `Play.unity` only through the builder and the setup menu items.

## Build

1. **Blocks.** `Check Building Blocks`, `Configure Building Blocks` and the Quest build gate work on Play.unity instead of Site.unity (today they are hard-wired to `SiteSceneBuilder.ScenePath` and require Real Hands). Check no longer requires Real Hands; it fails when it finds Real Hands, an `EnableUnpremultipliedAlpha` component, more than one `OVRPassthroughLayer`, or a demo block, and names what Bryan should remove in the Building Blocks window (K34). `Configure Building Blocks` works on Play.unity as before (locomotion and gravity off, floor-level origin) and also enables the passthrough layer at start and gives the centre eye camera a transparent background (solid colour, alpha 0) with HDR off. It changes values only.
2. **Builder.** `LOB Over > Build > Play Scene` opens Play.unity and rebuilds only `[LOBOver]` roots: Lighting; Hub (a floor collider with its top at y = 0, and the panel); Site (the full-scale world, inactive at start, without the truck, the cab or the plan panel); Client (connection and voice); Warp; Preview (the desktop preview for Editor screenshots, excluded from builds). Block objects are never touched. Running it twice changes nothing else.
3. **Client.** A `HostConnection` replaces the embedded simulation in Play.unity, in the Editor and on the headset. `ClientWebSocket` to `ws://<host>:<port>/ws`; `hello` as seat `site`, client `quest`. Finding the host: try `127.0.0.1:7331` (USB with `adb reverse`) for 1 s; otherwise listen on UDP 7332 for `LOBOVER 1 <port> <hostname>` and connect to the sender; after a loss, retry every 2 s. `state` messages become the same `SiteView` the world presenter uses now; `voice` messages queue for playback; commands go out as `cmd`. Android: the INTERNET, ACCESS_WIFI_STATE and CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE permissions, a multicast lock while listening, cleartext traffic to local addresses allowed.
4. **Hub.** Full passthrough; nothing is drawn except the panel. The panel appears once, after the first valid head pose, 0.55 m ahead and 0.15 m below eye height, tilted to face the eyes, within reach of a finger; it does not follow, and it re-places itself after a system recenter. 0.40 m wide; opaque dark background; title text at least 2.5 cm tall, other text at least 1.5 cm; buttons at least 11 by 6 cm. Content: "LOB, Over"; one status line ("Looking for the game. Start it on the PC with Play LOB Over." / "Connected to <hostname>. Waiting for Plan to press Start." / "Day <n>"); three buttons, enabled only while connected: "Ray Â· digging", "Marcus Â· pipe", "Dana Â· backfill". Ray and poke, controllers and hands, through the Interaction SDK. A press plays a click and starts the warp at once.
5. **Warp** (`Art/Warp.shader`, URP, single-pass instanced; `WarpController`). In: read the chosen crew's current station; move the site root (never the rig or the camera) so that the road beside that station is under the player's feet at y = 0 and the trench lies ahead in the player's facing direction; enable the site behind an inward-facing sphere around the head that writes alpha 0, so passthrough still shows; over 1.0 s the sphere dissolves from the feet upward with a noise edge and a thin bright rim; then disable the passthrough layer and hide the hub. Out: the reverse, ending with the site hidden and the hub shown. Send `moveTo` (that station) and then `dismount`. Nothing moves during the dissolve. If the shader cannot load, use a 0.3 s fade through black.
6. **In the site.** The existing world presenter, driven by the host's views. Back on the left wrist (hand or controller), by poke or ray, warps out. Voice: each turn plays from its speaker's crew position (3D sound, clear within about 40 m, silent beyond about 120 m); exchanges never overlap, the next waits for the current one; an `arrive` line plays as it comes. Clips come from StreamingAssets as now (run Sync Data before the build so `Voice/level1_texas/` is included), one per turn as `<lineId>_<n>.wav`, with the scenario id taken from the host's `welcome`.
7. **Remove** from Play.unity and the Android build: the start menu (modes, scenarios, Autoplan, Start), MR Diorama, the truck and cab, and the embedded simulation. Delete code that nothing uses any more; the C# simulation project and its tests stay.
8. **Tests.** EditMode: the builder twice leaves the block objects untouched (instance ids, parents); Check Building Blocks fails on a test scene with Real Hands or two passthrough layers; the discovery parser reads a packet. PlayMode, against a Node host the test starts on port 7333 with `level1_texas.json`: it connects as `site` and receives `welcome` and a `state`; pressing Marcus through the Interaction SDK's event ends with the head within 0.5 m of the road beside pipe, exactly one `moveTo` sent, the site active and passthrough disabled; Back returns to the hub with passthrough enabled; the centre eye camera's background is transparent at start; the rig stays above y = -0.01 m for 3 s; a two-turn exchange plays its turns in order without overlap.

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: compile clean; Check and Configure Building Blocks pass; Play Scene twice; EditMode and PlayMode pass; Play in the Editor with `Play LOB Over.cmd` running shows Connected, a warp to Marcus (screenshot) and Back; Build and Run Quest; the host log shows the headset joining as `site`, client `quest`.
- Bryan, in the headset, with `Play LOB Over.cmd` running on the PC: (1) the whole room is visible and the panel is sharp and readable; (2) the panel says Connected; (3) touching Marcus dissolves the room into the site beside the pipe crew; (4) Back on the wrist dissolves it back into the room; (5) after Plan presses Start, the crews move and Marcus's lines play when the wall falls. Accepted only when all five pass.

## Out of scope

The table model, the PM standee, stake markers, Stop work, flags, confirmations, the Real Hands block, the GitHub Pages showcase.

## Amendment 1 (orchestrator, 2026-10-21): where Play.unity is

Bryan saved the scene at `Assets/Scenes/Play.unity` (not `Assets/LOBOver/Scenes/Play.unity`). Use that path everywhere this work order says Play.unity: the Check and Configure Building Blocks menu items, the Quest build gate, `LOB Over > Build > Play Scene`, the build list (`ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset`: Play.unity only), and the tests. Do not move or rename it. "You may change" therefore includes `Assets/Scenes/Play.unity`, only through the builder and the setup menu items, exactly as before. Orchestrator's MCP read of it on October 21: roots `Directional Light`, `[BuildingBlock] Camera Rig` (holding Controller Tracking left and right, Hand Tracking left and right and `[BuildingBlock] OVRComprehensiveInteractionRig`), `[BuildingBlock] Passthrough` (the only `OVRPassthroughLayer`, enabled); no Real Hands, no `EnableUnpremultipliedAlpha`, no demo block.
# WO-L8 The site in front of you

## Goal

One warp, one site, everything in view. From the hub a single button builds the site around the player with the wireframe effect; the three crews work in front of the player at real scale, grounded, with the pipe crew in the middle and the others nearer or farther as their real gaps change; the two foremen argue in front of the player; what anyone says appears in a speech bubble over the speaker and in a log panel the player can open and move; each simulated day passes as a sunrise-to-dusk cycle with a short dusk at the day boundary. Bryan's words on the fourth build: the equipment is extremely small and sometimes floating; all the action happens on my left; without the text it is hard to understand what is going on; three buttons made me expect three places when one site is enough.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md`, `Docs/LEVEL1.md` ("In the headset", rewritten October 22), `Docs/VOICE.md` ("Principles"), `Docs/DECISIONS.md` K45 to K48, WO-L4 (ground, SiteLit, props), WO-L5 (the wave), WO-L7 (head camera, no tunnel)
- `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/Play/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/World/**` (CrewRig, StationCell, SiteWorld, SiteSky, WorldAssets), `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/{PlaySceneBuilder,SiteAssetBuilder,SiteGeometryBuilder}.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`
- The Interaction SDK's `Grabbable`, `HandGrabInteractable`, `GrabInteractable`, `OneGrabTranslateTransformer`, `PointableCanvas`

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Play.unity` only through the builder. Never a `[BuildingBlock]` object, never `Assets/LOBGame/**`. The simulation, the host and the protocol stay as they are; the headset sends only the commands `PROTOCOL.md` already has.

## Build

1. **Hub.** The panel keeps its status line and gets one button, "Go to the site", in place of the three crew buttons. The Back button on the wrist returns to the hub as now.
2. **Stage layout.** On entry the site root is placed so the player stands on the road with the trench 6 m in front, running left to right across the view, excavation to the right (the direction of work), backfill to the left. The player's facing direction at the moment of the warp defines "front"; the site is placed by the tracked head position (the head's x and z, the floor at y = 0). Crew slots along the trench, measured from the point straight ahead of the player: pipe at 0; excavation at +(8 m + 0.02 m per foot of real gap), capped at +30 m; backfill at âˆ’(8 m + 0.02 m per foot of real gap), capped at âˆ’30 m; a gap of 0 ft (backfill has caught pipe) puts backfill at âˆ’4 m. Slots move with the simulation at no more than 1 m/s (the machines crawl, the player never moves). The trench cells in front take their look from the real cells around each front: undug beyond excavation, open between excavation and pipe (with the collapse and water at the pipe front when the event is live), piped between pipe and backfill, backfilled beyond backfill. The station stake nearest each crew shows that crew's real station (`5+20`) in 6 cm letters; the stakes between them are unlabelled. The rest of the site (road, spoil, fences, props, trees, sky, haze) stays as WO-L4 built it, extending at least 120 m each way so the view never ends.
3. **Scale and grounding.** Machines at real size, set by the builder from each prefab's bounds: Standard excavator 9.5 m long, 3.2 m to the cab roof; Large 11 m and 3.6 m; Small 7.5 m and 2.8 m; Standard crane 12 m and 3.8 m; Heavy 14 m and 4.2 m; backfill loaders 6 m long and 3.2 m tall (Small 5 m, Large 7 m); pipe 0.3 m outside diameter in 6 m lengths; workers 1.8 m. Every machine, worker, pile and prop rests on the ground: the builder and the runtime place each by the ground height function the ground mesh was generated from (`GroundHeight(x, z)`), lowest point of the bounds on the surface, machines on the far bank, workers beside the trench. Nothing floats and nothing sinks; a test checks every placed object's bounds against the ground within 5 cm.
4. **Attention.** Looking at a crew for 2 s (gaze dwell on its machine or workers) sends `moveTo` for that crew's real station, so the host's position logic and the `arrive` lines follow attention; a thin ring under the crew brightens while it is the attended one. On entry the attended crew is pipe.
5. **Speech.** A `voice` turn plays from its speaker's position as now, and for its duration plus 2 s a speech bubble floats 0.4 m above the speaker's head: the speaker's name in the crew colour and the text in white on an opaque dark card, billboarded to the player, scaled with distance so the cap height stays about 2 cm at 10 m, at most two bubbles at once (the exchange's current and previous turn). The two arguing foremen meet at the spot between their slots and face each other, as the ConflictTalk animation does now.
6. **Log panel.** A world-locked panel, 0.5 m by 0.35 m, opaque, that opens in front of the player (1 m ahead, 0.15 m below eye height, facing the eyes, placed from the head pose at the moment it opens) from a wrist button "Log", and closes from the same button or its own close button. It lists the last twenty lines in order: day stamp, speaker in the crew colour, text; it also shows the current day and the clock state (Running, Field slow). The player can grab it with a controller or a hand (Interaction SDK `Grabbable` with `HandGrabInteractable` and `GrabInteractable`, translate and rotate, no scale) and leave it anywhere; it stays where it is left and reopens where it was unless it is out of view, in which case it is re-placed in front. Text 1.5 cm cap height or more.
7. **Day and night.** The sun travels from 20Â° above the eastern horizon to 20Â° above the western over each simulated day (the sun elevation follows the fraction of the day; the directional light and the sky dome follow the sun), and the day boundary is a dusk: over the last 15% of the day the light warms and dims to 25% and the sky deepens, then the new day opens at dawn brightness within 0.3 s, with the day number shown for 1.5 s as a 20 cm caption 2 m in front of the player ("Day 6"). The same at the slow pace (15 s a day). Never fully dark, and no flashes faster than 0.3 s.
8. **The wave, stronger.** The warp in runs 2.5 s and the blueprint band is 14 m wide, so the wire-and-blueprint phase is readable on the machines and the people in front before the surfaces fill; the warp out runs 1.5 s. A machine or worker that enters the view later (a crew whose slot was beyond the stage) spawns with the same wire-to-surface effect over 0.6 s in place.
9. **Tests.** EditMode: each machine prefab's bounds match the sizes above within 10%; every placed object rests on the ground within 5 cm. PlayMode against the host on 7333 at Day 5: the three crews are in front of the simulated head (within Â±60Â° of the facing direction) at the slot distances the formula gives; excavation is to the right of pipe and backfill to the left; a `voice` message shows a bubble above the speaker and a log line; the Log button opens the panel within 1.2 m of the head and facing it; gaze dwell on the excavation crew sends one `moveTo` for its station; a day boundary dims the light to about 25% and back; the hub has exactly one crew-independent button. Screenshots through the head camera (WO-L7): the hub panel; the site at entry; the argument; the log panel open; a dusk frame.

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: compile clean; Play Scene twice; tests pass; the head-camera screenshots reviewed against the goal sentence (all three crews in front and at real scale, nothing floating, a bubble readable, the panel in reach, the dusk); no console errors; stats within the WO-L4 budget. No Quest build.
- Bryan, after building: the site builds around him with the wire phase visible; the three crews work in front of him at real size on the ground; Marcus's words appear over his head and in the log; the panel opens in front and can be grabbed and moved; the days pass as dusks; the argument happens in front of him.

## Out of scope

Stop work, flags, confirmations, the table model, stake-to-stake warps, the Plan page.

## Amendment 1 (October 22, K50, after rounds 0 to 2)

Bryan's rulings, which replace the matching numbers above:

- The trench runs across 7 m in front of the player (item 2 said 6 m); the machines stand on the far bank at real size, the near edge of each machine within 3 m of the far trench edge.
- The crew slot cap is 18 m instead of 30 m (item 2), so the excavator stays at the edge of the view; the formula is otherwise unchanged.
- The far-bank check, in the builder, at runtime and in the tests, measures the lowest 1.5 m of each machine's bounds (tracks, wheels, body), never the boom.
- Workers keep their imported meshes: no mesh reduction on skinned meshes (the fence batching may stay).
- The small excavator is scaled to 2.8 m to the cab roof within 10 percent like the other variants.
- The Day-5 "in front" check of the PlayMode test is within 65 degrees of the facing direction (rejection 1).

## Amendment 2 (October 22, K51 to K53, after round 3)

- K51: the stage budget is 500,000 triangles and 150 draw calls in every view the player can look at (the hub keeps the 300,000 guideline); workers keep their meshes; a distance LOD for workers and machines is a follow-up only if the Quest 3S frame time shows it. The budget tests assert 500,000 and 150 for the stage views and log each number.
- K52: grounding and the far-bank rule read only the renderers whose bounds touch the ground (lowest point within 0.3 m of the surface); booms, hooks, cables and animated parts are excluded from the placement checks and from the bounds-consistency test.
- K53: a machine is scaled by one dimension, its length; the roof height is reported, not asserted.



